# C3 (REVIEW-2026-10-30): teaching explanations for A1–A2 grammar + vocabulary.
# q -> (explain = rule + example, [note for each wrong option, in the order the wrong options appear in the item])
C3A = {
"a1/grammar": {
"I ___ a student.": ("With I we always use am: I am, you are, he/she/it is. Example: I am happy.", ["Is goes with he, she or it: She is a student.", "Are goes with you, we and they: You are a student.", "Be needs a helper word first: I can be a student."]),
"She ___ two brothers.": ("With he, she or it we use has, not have. Example: Tom has a cat.", ["Have goes with I, you, we and they: I have two brothers.", "There is no word 'haves'. The -s form of have is has.", "Having needs is/are first, and we don't use it for family."]),
"They ___ from Spain.": ("They takes are: we are, you are, they are. Example: They are my friends.", ["Is is for one person or thing: He is from Spain.", "Am only goes with I: I am from Spain.", "Be needs a helper word: They will be in Spain."]),
"This is ___ apple.": ("Use an before a vowel sound (a, e, i, o, u). Example: an egg, an orange.", ["A goes before a consonant sound: a banana. Apple starts with a vowel.", "Two needs a plural word: two apples.", "Many needs a plural word too: many apples."]),
"___ is your name?": ("We ask about a name with What. Example: What is your name? – I'm Lily.", ["Who asks about a person: Who is she? – My sister.", "Where asks about a place: Where is your bag?", "When asks about a time: When is your birthday?"]),
"I ___ like coffee.": ("With I, you, we, they the 'not' word is don't. Example: I don't like rain.", ["Doesn't is for he, she, it: She doesn't like coffee.", "We can't say 'I not like'. We need don't.", "No goes before a noun: I have no coffee."]),
"He ___ football every Sunday.": ("For things he/she/it does often, add -s to the verb. Example: She reads every day.", ["Play is for I, you, we, they: We play football.", "Playing needs is: He is playing now.", "We don't say 'is play'. Every Sunday = present simple: plays."]),
"There ___ three cats in the garden.": ("There are for more than one thing; there is for one. Example: There are two dogs.", ["There is is for one thing: There is one cat.", "Am only goes with I.", "Be needs a helper word: There will be three cats."]),
"The book is ___ the table.": ("On means touching the top of something. Example: The cup is on the desk.", ["At is for a point or place: at school, at the door.", "To shows movement: I go to the park.", "From shows where something starts: a letter from Gran."]),
"Can you ___?": ("After can, use the plain verb with no -s, no -ing and no to. Example: I can sing.", ["No -s after can: we say 'Can he swim?', not 'swims'.", "No -ing after can: 'Can you swimming?' is wrong.", "No 'to' after can: 'Can you to swim?' is wrong."]),
"These are my ___.": ("These means more than one, so the noun is plural. Example: These are my socks.", ["Shoe is just one. These needs more than one: shoes.", "Shoe takes just -s: shoes, not 'shoeses'.", "A shoe is one shoe. These needs a plural."]),
"___ you like ice cream?": ("Questions with you/I/we/they and a normal verb start with Do. Example: Do you like cats?", ["Does is for he, she, it: Does she like ice cream?", "Are goes with adjectives or -ing: Are you happy?", "Is goes with he/she/it: Is it cold?"]),
"My birthday is ___ May.": ("Use in with months, years and seasons. Example: in June, in winter.", ["On is for days and dates: on Monday, on 5th May.", "At is for clock times: at six o'clock.", "To shows movement, not time."]),
"Look! The baby ___.": ("Look! means it is happening now, so use is + -ing. Example: Look! It is raining.", ["Sleep is for I/you/we/they and for habits, not now.", "Sleeps is for habits: The baby sleeps a lot.", "Are is for more than one. One baby takes is."]),
"This is Tom. ___ is my friend.": ("Tom is a boy, so we use he. Example: This is Sam. He is nine.", ["She is for a girl or woman.", "It is for a thing or an animal.", "They is for more than one person."]),
"We ___ at home yesterday.": ("Yesterday is past. The past of are is were (we were, they were). Example: We were happy.", ["Was is for I, he, she, it: I was at home.", "Are is now, not yesterday.", "Is is now and for one person."]),
"___ is my bag? I can't find it.": ("Where asks about a place. Example: Where is the cat? – Under the bed.", ["What asks about a thing: What is this?", "Who asks about a person: Who is that?", "How asks about the way or feelings: How are you?"]),
"My sister ___ like milk.": ("With he, she, it the 'not' word is doesn't. Example: He doesn't like rain.", ["Don't is for I, you, we, they.", "Isn't goes with adjectives: She isn't happy. Like is a verb.", "We can't say 'She not like'. We need doesn't."]),
"I have ___ umbrella.": ("Umbrella starts with a vowel sound, so we use an. Example: an ice cream.", ["A is for consonant sounds: a bag, a hat.", "Some is for 'more than one' or 'a bit of': some milk.", "Two needs a plural: two umbrellas."]),
"The cat is ___ the box. You can't see it!": ("In means inside something. Example: The pencil is in my bag.", ["On is on the top. Then you could see the cat!", "At is for a place point: at the door.", "Of is not used for where things are."]),
"___ is it? It's five o'clock.": ("We ask about the clock with What time. Example: What time is lunch? – At one.", ["How many asks about a number of things.", "How old asks about age.", "What day asks about Monday, Tuesday..."]),
"They ___ TV now.": ("Now means use are/is + -ing. They takes are. Example: They are playing.", ["Watch is for habits: They watch TV every day.", "Watches is for he/she/it habits.", "Is is for one person. They needs are."]),
"I get up ___ seven o'clock.": ("Use at with clock times. Example: at nine o'clock, at noon.", ["In is for months and years: in May.", "On is for days: on Friday.", "To shows movement, not a time."]),
"This is my brother. ___ name is Leo.": ("His shows something belongs to a boy. Example: Sam and his dog.", ["Her is for a girl: my sister and her name.", "He is the person, not 'belonging to him'. We need his.", "Its is for a thing or an animal."]),
"How ___ is your grandma? She's seventy.": ("We ask about age with How old. Example: How old are you? – I'm eight.", ["How many is for counting things.", "How much is for amounts and prices.", "How big asks about size, not age."]),
"There ___ a dragon in the story.": ("One thing takes there is. Example: There is a cat on the bed.", ["Are is for more than one: There are two dragons.", "Am only goes with I.", "Be needs a helper word first."]),
"I can ___ my name in English.": ("After can, use the plain verb. Example: She can write.", ["No -s after can.", "No -ing after can.", "No 'to' after can."]),
"___ she have a pet?": ("Questions with he/she/it and a normal verb start with Does. Example: Does he like fish?", ["Do is for I, you, we, they.", "Is goes with adjectives or -ing, not 'have'.", "Has is not used to start this question. Say 'Does she have'."]),
"Those ___ my pencils.": ("Those means more than one, so use are. Example: Those are my shoes.", ["Is is for one: That is my pencil.", "Am only goes with I.", "Be needs a helper word."]),
"Sam was tired, ___ he went to bed.": ("So shows a result: what happened because of it. Example: It was cold, so I wore a coat.", ["But shows a surprise or difference: tired, but he played.", "Because gives the reason, not the result: He went to bed because he was tired.", "Or gives a choice: tea or milk?"]),
},
"a1/vocab": {
"The opposite of 'big' is ___.": ("Big and small are opposites. Example: an elephant is big, a mouse is small.", ["Tall is about height: a tall tree. Its opposite is short.", "Long is about length. Its opposite is short.", "Old is about age. Its opposite is new or young."]),
"Monday, Tuesday, ___": ("The days go Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday.", ["Thursday comes after Wednesday.", "Friday comes later in the week.", "Sunday is the last day of the week."]),
"You sleep in a ___. [🛏️]": ("A bed is for sleeping. Example: I go to bed at eight.", ["A cup is for drinking.", "A door is for going in and out.", "A window is for looking out."]),
"Which number is 'twelve'? [🔢]": ("Twelve is 12 (ten and two). Example: There are 12 months in a year.", ["20 is twenty.", "2 is two.", "11 is eleven."]),
"A person who cooks food is a ___. [🧑‍🍳]": ("A cook makes food. Example: The cook made soup.", ["A doctor helps sick people.", "A teacher works in a school.", "A driver drives a car or bus."]),
"The opposite of 'hot' is ___.": ("Hot and cold are opposites. Example: Ice is cold, soup is hot.", ["Warm is a little hot, not the opposite.", "Wet is the opposite of dry.", "New is the opposite of old."]),
"You wear a ___ on your head.": ("A hat goes on your head. Example: a sun hat.", ["A shoe goes on your foot.", "A sock goes on your foot too.", "A belt goes around your waist."]),
"Red, blue and yellow are ___.": ("Red, blue and yellow are colours. Example: My favourite colour is green.", ["Numbers are one, two, three...", "Animals are cats, dogs...", "Days are Monday, Tuesday..."]),
"Your mum's mum is your ___.": ("Your mum's mum or dad's mum is your grandma. Example: Grandma bakes cakes.", ["An aunt is your mum's or dad's sister.", "A sister has the same mum as you.", "A cousin is your aunt's or uncle's child."]),
"We eat ___ in the morning.": ("The morning meal is breakfast. Example: I have toast for breakfast.", ["Dinner is the evening meal.", "Lunch is the middle of the day.", "Supper is a late evening meal."]),
"January, February, ___": ("The months start January, February, March, April...", ["May comes after April.", "Monday is a day, not a month.", "Spring is a season, not a month."]),
"A person who teaches at a school is a ___.": ("A teacher teaches. Example: Our teacher reads us stories.", ["A doctor helps sick people.", "A farmer works on a farm.", "A driver drives a car or bus."]),
},
"a2/grammar": {
"I ___ to the cinema last night.": ("Last night is a finished past time, so use the past simple: go → went. Example: I went home.", ["Go is the present.", "Have gone can't go with a finished time like 'last night'.", "Goes is present, for he/she/it."]),
"This cake is ___ than that one.": ("Short adjectives add -er to compare: sweet → sweeter. Example: A cat is smaller than a dog.", ["More is for long adjectives: more beautiful. Sweet is short.", "Sweetest compares three or more: the sweetest.", "With 'than' we need the comparative form."]),
"How ___ apples do you need?": ("How many is for things we can count. Example: How many eggs?", ["How much is for things we can't count: How much milk?", "Lot needs 'a lot of', and doesn't follow 'how'.", "Any is not used after 'how'."]),
"We ___ for the bus when it finally arrived.": ("A longer past action that was interrupted uses was/were + -ing. Example: I was reading when you called.", ["Are waiting is the present.", "Wait is the present simple.", "Have waited doesn't fit a finished past story."]),
"I've lived here ___ three years.": ("For + a length of time; since + a starting point. Example: for two weeks, since Monday.", ["Since needs a starting point: since 2023.", "From needs 'to': from 2020 to 2023.", "During means inside a period: during the summer."]),
"You ___ wear a coat. It's warm today.": ("Don't have to = it isn't necessary. Example: You don't have to come, but you can.", ["Mustn't means it is not allowed. Wearing a coat isn't wrong!", "Have to means it IS necessary, but it's warm.", "Must also means it is necessary."]),
"It's the ___ shop in the village.": ("Good → better → best. Example: She is my best friend.", ["Goodest isn't a word. Good is irregular.", "Better compares two things.", "Most good isn't used. Say best."]),
"Did you ___ the new bakery?": ("After did, use the plain verb. Example: Did you see it?", ["Did already shows the past, so no -ed.", "No -ing after did.", "No -s after did."]),
"If it rains, we ___ stay inside.": ("First conditional: if + present, will + verb. Example: If you call, I will come.", ["Would is for unreal situations: If I had wings, I would fly.", "Are can't go before 'stay' like this.", "Did is past."]),
"No more cake for me, thanks. I ___ already eaten three slices!": ("Present perfect: have + past participle, often with already. Example: I have already finished.", ["Did doesn't go with 'eaten'.", "Am doesn't go with 'eaten'.", "Was eaten would mean the slices ate you!"]),
"When I was young, I ___ climb trees every day.": ("Used to + verb = a past habit that has stopped. Example: I used to have long hair.", ["After 'I' in the past it's used to, with a d.", "Was used means someone used you.", "Am used to means 'I'm comfortable with' now."]),
"Is there ___ bread left?": ("In questions we usually use any. Example: Is there any milk?", ["Some is mostly in positive sentences: There is some bread.", "Many is for countable plurals: many apples.", "A is for one countable thing. Bread is uncountable."]),
"He runs ___ than his brother.": ("Fast → faster. Example: A horse is faster than a dog.", ["Fast is short, so no 'more'.", "Fastest compares three or more.", "Most fast isn't used. Say fastest."]),
"Look at those clouds! It ___ rain.": ("Going to = a prediction from what we can see now. Example: Look! He's going to fall!", ["We say 'will rain', never 'will to'.", "Goes to is a movement: He goes to school.", "Is raining to isn't English."]),
"I ___ a shower when the phone rang.": ("Past continuous for the longer action, past simple for the short one. Example: I was eating when he arrived.", ["Had would mean the shower came after the call.", "Have had is present perfect, not a story.", "Am having is now."]),
"We ___ go to school on Saturdays. It's the weekend!": ("Don't have to = not necessary. Example: I don't have to get up early on Sunday.", ["Mustn't means it's not allowed. Going isn't wrong!", "Haven't needs a past participle: haven't gone.", "We say 'can't go', with no 'to'."]),
"This is the girl ___ won the baking contest.": ("Who is for people. Example: the boy who sings.", ["Which is for things and animals.", "Where is for places.", "What isn't used to join a person to a sentence."]),
"She's ___ excited to sleep!": ("Too + adjective + to = more than is OK. Example: It's too hot to play.", ["Enough comes after the adjective: old enough.", "Very much doesn't go before an adjective.", "So much doesn't go before an adjective."]),
"Have you finished your homework ___?": ("Yet goes at the end of questions and negatives. Example: Has he arrived yet?", ["Already usually goes in the middle of a positive sentence.", "Still is for something that hasn't stopped.", "Ever asks 'at any time in your life'."]),
},
"a2/vocab": {
"You buy bread at a ___.": ("A bakery makes and sells bread and cakes. Example: The bakery smells lovely.", ["A pharmacy sells medicine.", "A library lends books.", "A bank keeps money."]),
"I feel ___. I need to go to bed.": ("Tired means you need sleep. Example: I'm tired after football.", ["Hungry means you need food.", "Angry means cross, not sleepy.", "Thirsty means you need a drink."]),
"The opposite of 'cheap' is ___.": ("Cheap costs little; expensive costs a lot. Example: Diamonds are expensive.", ["Free costs nothing, even less than cheap.", "Easy is the opposite of difficult.", "Heavy is the opposite of light."]),
"A person who serves food in a restaurant is a ___.": ("A waiter brings food to your table. Example: The waiter brought our pizza.", ["A chef cooks the food in the kitchen.", "A farmer grows food.", "A pilot flies planes."]),
"Can you ___ me some money? I'll pay you back.": ("Lend = give for a while. Borrow = take for a while. Example: Can I borrow your pen? / Can you lend me your pen?", ["Borrow is what YOU do: Can I borrow some money?", "Spend means use money to buy things.", "Owe means you must pay someone back."]),
"I ___ my homework every evening.": ("We say do homework. Example: Did you do your homework?", ["We make a cake, but we do homework.", "Take is for photos or buses.", "Play is for games and sports."]),
"We ___ a photo of the castle.": ("We take a photo. Example: Take a picture of me!", ["We do homework, not photos.", "Make a photo isn't natural English.", "Had a photo means owned one."]),
"The train was ___, so we arrived late.": ("Delayed means later than planned. Example: Our flight was delayed.", ["Early would make you arrive early.", "Fast would make you arrive early too.", "Empty means no people, not late."]),
"Which one is a vegetable?": ("A carrot is a vegetable. Example: Rabbits love carrots.", ["A strawberry is a fruit.", "A cherry is a fruit.", "A lemon is a fruit."]),
"Put on your ___ — it's raining!": ("A raincoat keeps you dry. Example: Take your raincoat to school.", ["Sunglasses are for sunny days.", "A swimsuit is for swimming.", "Sandals are for hot days."]),
"My mother's brother is my ___.": ("Your mum's or dad's brother is your uncle. Example: Uncle Ben tells jokes.", ["A cousin is your uncle's child.", "A nephew is your brother's or sister's son.", "A grandfather is your mum's or dad's dad."]),
"I'm very ___ in history. I love old castles.": ("-ed words say how you feel; -ing words describe the thing. Example: I'm interested in this interesting book.", ["Interesting describes the thing: History is interesting.", "Bored doesn't fit 'I love old castles'.", "Boring describes a thing, and doesn't fit."]),
"Could you ___ the window? It's hot in here.": ("Open the window to let air in. Example: Open the door, please.", ["Close would make it hotter.", "We turn a key or a tap, not a window.", "Break would be naughty!"]),
"The film was really ___. I laughed a lot.": ("Funny makes you laugh. Example: a funny clown.", ["Sad makes you cry.", "Scary makes you afraid.", "Boring doesn't make you laugh."]),
"A ___ is where you can see old and interesting things.": ("A museum shows old and interesting things. Example: We saw dinosaur bones at the museum.", ["A stadium is for sport and concerts.", "An airport is for planes.", "A garage is for cars."]),
"I need to ___ the bus at the next stop.": ("Get off = leave a bus or train. Example: Get off at the park.", ["Get on means go into the bus.", "Get up means leave your bed.", "Get in is for cars, not buses."]),
"Turn ___ the light, please. It's dark.": ("Turn on = start a light or machine. Example: Turn on the TV.", ["Turn off makes it darker.", "Turn up is for volume.", "Turn down is for volume or an offer."]),
"I'm ___. Can I have a glass of water?": ("Thirsty means you need a drink. Example: Running makes me thirsty.", ["Hungry means you need food.", "Sleepy means you need sleep.", "Angry is a feeling, not about water."]),
"We ___ our holiday in Scotland last year.": ("We spend time somewhere. Example: I spent the day at the beach.", ["Passed isn't used with holiday like this.", "Make a holiday isn't natural.", "Stayed needs 'in': We stayed in Scotland."]),
"Don't forget to ___ your teeth before bed.": ("We brush our teeth. Example: Brush your teeth twice a day.", ["We wash our hands or face.", "Clean up is for a messy room.", "We comb our hair."]),
"The shop ___ at six o'clock, so hurry!": ("Shops open and close. Example: The bank closes at five.", ["A film or lesson finishes, not a shop.", "A bus stops; a shop closes.", "End up means arrive somewhere in the end."]),
"A ___ is a doctor for animals.": ("A vet looks after sick animals. Example: We took our cat to the vet.", ["A nurse helps doctors with people.", "A dentist looks after teeth.", "A chemist sells medicine."]),
"I can't find my glasses. Can you help me ___ for them?": ("Look for = try to find. Example: I'm looking for my keys.", ["See doesn't go with 'for'.", "Watch is for TV or games.", "Find doesn't go with 'for'. Find them."]),
"The opposite of 'noisy' is ___.": ("Noisy and quiet are opposites. Example: Be quiet in the library.", ["Empty is the opposite of full.", "Slow is the opposite of fast.", "Calm down is a verb: Calm down, please."]),
"He ___ a cold, so he stayed at home.": ("We have a cold. Example: I have a cold and a sore throat.", ["We don't make a cold.", "We take medicine, not a cold.", "We don't do a cold."]),
"Which one do you wear on your hands in winter?": ("Gloves go on your hands. Example: Wear gloves in the snow.", ["Boots go on your feet.", "Scarves go around your neck.", "Belts go around your waist."]),
"Can I ___ on this dress? — Yes, the changing room is there.": ("Try on = put on clothes to see if they fit. Example: Try on these shoes.", ["Put on is for getting dressed, not testing in a shop.", "Wear doesn't go with 'on' here.", "Dress doesn't go with 'on this dress'."]),
"It's very ___ today. Hold on to your hat!": ("Windy = lots of wind. Example: It's windy, let's fly a kite!", ["Sunny wouldn't blow your hat away.", "Foggy means you can't see far.", "Snowing is a verb: It's snowing."]),
"My grandma is ___. She's 92!": ("Elderly is a kind word for old people. Example: an elderly neighbour.", ["Young is the opposite.", "Modern is for things, not people's age.", "Early is about time, not age."]),
"Please ___ the form and give it back to me.": ("Fill in a form = write the answers. Example: Fill in your name.", ["Fill up is for a glass or a car's tank.", "Write on a form isn't how we say it.", "Put in means place inside."]),
"I'd like to ___ a table for two at eight.": ("Book a table = reserve it. Example: We booked a table for Gran's birthday.", ["Order is for food: I'd like to order pizza.", "Keep means not give away.", "You can't buy a restaurant table!"]),
},
}
